2186512682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512855 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512855 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512855 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512855 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512855 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f02d209a-9adb-5732-8214-dc18cb0b1819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+4z=4$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3250257a-998e-56c2-b2b7-fdd8d8e6863b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512883 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+4z=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,4⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda \\\\ -2 &=4\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\mu x &=0 \\\\ \\lambda &=1 \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the first equation above, we must have $x=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(1) & =4 \\\\ y+4 & =4 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(-1) & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =4 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0,1),\\,(0,8,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,8,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,8,-1),$ and its  maximum value is $10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186512970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "272e8559-390b-50dd-a1a1-013d8cdb1fa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512985 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512985 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5255935c-1292-5a9d-9614-94e19bc8466b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512985 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186401398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "657450a0-d1cc-5cbf-b267-5bba4ceaf67a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186401391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512997 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6f6d8